<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bagrat VI of Georgia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1478">1478</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Bagrat II of Imereti, Bagrat of Imereti · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bagrat VI was a prince of a branch of the Georgian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a> who made himself king of Imereti in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. In 1463 he defeated King George VIII at Chikhori, a major step in the break-up of the united kingdom, and after George's capture by the atabeg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a> in 1465 he seized eastern Georgia as well, ruling as king of Georgia until his death in 1478.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bagrat-vi-of-georgia.jpg" alt="Bagrat VI of Georgia" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bagrat_VI_of_Georgia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bagrat was born around 1435, the son of Prince George, youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. Around 1455 King George VIII made him Eristavi (duke) of Samokalako, the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a> and western Imereti. In the early 1460s Bagrat supported the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qvarqvare-ii-jaqeli">Qvarqvare II Jaqeli</a>, atabeg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a>, and the king deprived him of his duchy. In 1463 he led the coalition of western Georgian nobles that defeated George VIII at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chikhori">Battle of Chikhori</a>, then captured Kutaisi and was crowned king of Imereti.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In return for their help Bagrat had to create a principality for each of his four allies. From then on the Gelovani ruled Svaneti, the Shervashidze Abkhazia, the Dadiani Odishi (Mingrelia) and the Vardanidze Guria as semi-independent princes. When George VIII was defeated and imprisoned by Qvarqvare of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a> in 1465, Bagrat crossed into Inner Kartli and proclaimed himself King of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, though in fact he held only western Georgia and Inner Kartli and stayed mostly in the west.</p><p>In his western lands he set up a separate church, the Catholicosate of Abkhazia, independent of the Patriarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a>. To justify it he asked Michael IV, Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, to compose a Law of Faith stating that western and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> had been converted separately. Once freed, George VIII held only Kakheti, while his nephew Constantine established himself in Lower Kartli, so that Georgia was divided among three rulers.</p><p>During these years <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> of the Ak Koyunlu attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> at least twice, and Bagrat had to make peace by abandoning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> recovered only after Uzun Hasan's death. In 1477 Vameq II Dadiani, eristav of Odishi, raised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abkhaz-people">Abkhazians</a> and Gurians and raided Imereti, but Bagrat defeated and subdued him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bagrat died in 1478 and was buried at the Gelati Monastery near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a>. His wife Helen outlived him until 3 November 1510. Of their three sons, Alexander II succeeded him and, after a brief failed attempt on the throne, ruled as King of Imereti from 1484 to 1510.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bagrat VI” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Regional lords pressed for independence: <b>Bagrat of Imereti</b> defeated George VIII at Chikhori in 1463, and the atabeg Qvarqvare II of Samtskhe captured him in 1465.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
